.TH IOURING_MONITOR 1 "September 2026" "ebpf-syscall" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
iouring_monitor \- record io_uring submissions and completions with eBPF
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iouring_monitor
.RB [ \-\-pid " PID" ]
.RB [ \-\-dur " SECONDS" ]
.RB [ \-\-jsonl " PATH" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B iouring_monitor
attaches to io_uring tracepoints and records submission and completion events.
It can restrict process-scoped events to one process and writes a final drop
record so consumers can reject an incomplete capture.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-pid " PID"
Restrict the capture to one thread-group ID.
.TP
.BI \-\-dur " seconds"
Stop after the given duration.  Otherwise stop with SIGINT or SIGTERM.
.TP
.BI \-\-jsonl " path"
Write newline-delimited JSON to the path instead of standard output.
.SH FIDELITY
This observes io_uring, not the NVMe device.  Filesystems and the block layer
can split, merge, cache, or reorder requests below this point.  Use
.BR nvme_tp_monitor (1)
when the device command stream is the required witness.
.SH PRIVACY
The trace contains process and request metadata.  It does not copy IO payloads,
but identifiers, lengths, and timing can still reveal workload structure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kvio (1),
.BR nvme_uring_cmd_monitor (1),
.BR nvme_tp_monitor (1)
